<h2><strong>Frequently Asked Questions</strong></h2>
<h3><strong>Can I wear contact lenses if I have astigmatism?</strong></h3>
<p>In most cases, yes. Mild to moderate astigmatism is usually correctable with a soft toric lens. Higher or irregular astigmatism often needs a toric RGP or hybrid lens, which holds its shape more precisely than a soft lens can.</p>
<h3><strong>What is the best contact lens option for keratoconus?</strong></h3>
<p>Scleral lenses are the most common recommendation because they vault over the irregular cornea entirely and rest on the sclera, avoiding contact with the thinned or bulging area. RGP lenses are also used for milder cases, depending on the severity of the keratoconus.</p>
<h3><strong>Why do my contacts feel uncomfortable even though my prescription is correct?</strong></h3>
<p>Discomfort usually points to a fit or tear film issue rather than a prescription problem. A lens can be optically correct and still sit poorly on the eye or dry out faster than your tear film can keep up with, both of which a standard exam without topography can miss.</p>
<h3><strong>I was told I'm not a candidate for contacts. Is that actually true?</strong></h3>
<p>Not always, and often not at all. That conclusion is often based on a limited set of lens types being tried rather than a full evaluation of your specific corneal needs. A hard-to-fit consultation with corneal mapping can identify options a standard fitting would never reach.</p>
<h3><strong>Are scleral lenses right for me?</strong></h3>
<p>Scleral lenses tend to work well for keratoconus, post-surgical corneas, irregular astigmatism, and moderate to severe dry eye. Whether they are the right choice for your eyes specifically depends on your corneal shape and tear film, which the fitting evaluation assesses.</p>
<h3><strong>How long does a hard-to-fit contact lens fitting take?</strong></h3>
<p>The initial evaluation and trial lens fitting typically takes longer than a standard contact lens exam, often close to an hour. Most patients need at least one follow-up visit before the final lenses are ordered.</p>
